#!lm
MODEL=litellm_proxy/anthropic/Claude Opus 5
CONTEXT=urn:cts:latinLit:phi0474.phi016.omar:1.1.t106-urn:cts:latinLit:phi0474.phi016.omar:1.1.t149
REASONING=Structure: main clause "huic profecto ipsi ... et opem et salutem ferre debemus" (debemus = independent verb, with ferre as complementary infinitive). Conditional protasis "si haec vox ... saluti fuit" (fuit = dependent linking verb, subordinated by si -> debemus). "conformata" is a perfect passive participle agreeing with "vox"; since it has a predicate/circumstantial sense ("having been shaped by his encouragement and teachings"), it anchors its own dependent verbal expression, with "vox" as its antecedent via 'circumstantial participle'. (This is a close call — it could be read attributively with "vox" — but per the rule preferring the circumstantial reading, and because of the ablatives of means attached to it, I take it as circumstantial.) "a quo id accepimus" is a relative clause whose antecedent is the postponed "huic ... ipsi"; "accepimus" is active, so "a" here is source, not agent: it is adverbial to "accepimus", with "quo" as its object of preposition plus the relative-pronoun link to "huic". "quo ceteris opitulari et alios servare possemus" is a relative/purpose clause with ablative-of-means "quo" (antecedent "id"); "possemus" is its dependent verb, with "opitulari" and "servare" as complementary infinitives (not verbal expressions of their own) coordinated by "et". "quantum est situm in nobis" is an inserted dependent clause; "quantum" is treated like a subordinating word pointing to "debemus", and the compound passive "situm est" is anchored at the participle "situm", with "est" as its auxiliary. "quod" in "quod si" I take as the adverbial/connecting accusative of respect relating to the main verb "debemus"; an alternative would be to treat it as a connecting relative pronoun with an external antecedent, but with no antecedent in the passage the accusative relation is preferable. "et opem et salutem": the first "et" is introductory (relatedtoken1 -> opem only), the second joins opem and salutem.

